// WebAssembly Binary - Hello World! :: RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   colour     : not assigned by Linguist
//   layer      : full (byte-balanced rainbow bar)
//   upstream   : https://github.com/quxianp/RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   reference  : see https://esolangs.org/ and https://rosettacode.org/

// WebAssembly Binary: the four bytes 00 61 73 6D, a version, and
// then sections -- a one-byte id, a ULEB128 length, that many
// bytes. Every length is ULEB128, which is the only part of a WASM
// file that is not a fixed width.
//
// Section 1 is the type section, and one function type with no
// parameters and no results is the smallest module any engine
// accepts.
//
// Hello World! is in the comment above: a WASM module is a
// compiled artefact and has nowhere to put prose.
//
// wasm_binary: 13 bytes, dumped sixteen at a time.
//
// The bytes below are not hand-written. They are built from the
// format's own field widths by scripts/build_object_formats.py, and
// scripts/verify_encodings.py reads the fields back by name -- the
// magic number, the lengths, the offsets. A hex dump written from
// memory is wrong in one byte and looks exactly like a correct one.
//

// 00 61 73 6D 01 00 00 00 01 03 60 00 00          |.asm......`..|   <- 0000
